From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 12:41:26 +0200
Subject: net: lantiq: gswip: build the PCE control word from zero

The four PCE indirect-table accessors -- gsw_pce_table_write,
gsw_pce_table_read, gsw_pce_table_key_read, gsw_pce_table_key_write --
each build PCE_TBL_CTRL out of a handful of gsw_field_w32 merges and
then write it back. What they merged onto was the register's current
contents, so every bit the sequence does not itself set was carried over
from whichever PCE transaction ran last. A read that leaves OPMOD, TYPE,
VLD or GMAP set hands those bits to an unrelated write on a different
sub-table.

AVM's 07.30 GPL release composes onto chip state the same way, though
not visibly on purpose: its busy-poll is a do/while around gsw_r32_raw,
so ctrlval is simply still holding whatever the poll last read when the
merges start. We had to reproduce that deliberately, because CHECK_BUSY
keeps its read-back in a macro-local, and the comment on the explicit
read argued the inheritance was intended -- specifically for the EXTOP
bit, which AVM never writes by name.

AVM's 08.25 GPL release refutes that reading. It inserts "ctrlval = 0"
immediately before the composition in all four accessors
(gsw_tbl_rw.c:512, :589, :674 and :735 in Linux 4.9.337), which is
exactly the state the old comment said AVM was avoiding. So the
re-seeding reads become "ctrlval = 0", and the comment now records that
the EXTOP argument did not survive the newer release rather than
continuing to make it.

Two reads that look identical are deliberately left alone: the ones after
the *second* CHECK_BUSY in gsw_pce_table_read and gsw_pce_table_key_read.
Those are not seeds for a write, they are the source for the gsw_field_r32
extractions of ptdata->type, ->valid and ->group. AVM gets that value free
from its do/while; we have to read it. Zeroing them would report all three
as zero on every read. Their comments already say so and are unchanged.

gsw_pce_table_key_read also gets AVM's ordering: all key registers are
written first, then the control word is composed in one run. Ours split the
composition around the key writes, putting the ADDR field before them and
the rest after, which only made sense while ctrlval had to be captured
before something else clobbered it. Behaviour is identical -- the key
registers are written before CTRL(BAS=1) either way.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/switch-api/gsw_flow_pce.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/switch-api/gsw_flow_pce.c
index c6dc469d30ed..d75cb1a9951a 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/switch-api/gsw_flow_pce.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/switch-api/gsw_flow_pce.c
@@ -149,13 +149,21 @@ int gsw_pce_table_write(void *cdev, pctbl_prog_t *ptdata)
 	CHECK_BUSY(PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_OFFSET, PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SHIFT,
 		   PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SIZE, RETURN_FROM_FUNCTION);
 	/*
-	 * Re-seed ctrlval from PCE_TBL_CTRL post-poll. Without this read,
-	 * gsw_field_w32(ctrlval, ...) would consume an uninitialized auto
-	 * variable - which both -Wuninitialized would flag fatal and would
-	 * write stack garbage into the PCE_TBL_CTRL register on the
-	 * subsequent gsw_w32_raw(..., ctrlval).
+	 * Compose the control word from zero, so every field this sequence
+	 * does not set is written as zero rather than inherited from whatever
+	 * the previous PCE transaction left in the register.
+	 *
+	 * AVM's 07.30 drop has no explicit seed: its do/while busy-poll leaves
+	 * ctrlval holding the register value it last read, and the
+	 * gsw_field_w32 calls below merge onto that. CHECK_BUSY keeps its
+	 * read-back in a macro-local we cannot see, so this used to be an
+	 * explicit re-seeding read, on the reading that composing onto chip
+	 * state was deliberate - notably for the EXTOP bit, which AVM never
+	 * writes explicitly. The 08.25 drop refutes that reading: it inserts
+	 * ctrlval = 0 before the composition in all four accessors
+	 * (gsw_tbl_rw.c:512, :589, :674, :735). Follow it.
 	 */
-	gsw_r32_raw(cdev, PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_OFFSET, &ctrlval);
+	ctrlval = 0;
 
 	gsw_w32_raw(cdev, PCE_TBL_ADDR_ADDR_OFFSET, ptdata->pcindex);
 	/*TABLE ADDRESS*/
@@ -235,8 +243,8 @@ int gsw_pce_table_read(void *cdev, pctbl_prog_t *ptdata)
 
 	CHECK_BUSY(PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_OFFSET, PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SHIFT,
 		   PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SIZE, RETURN_FROM_FUNCTION);
-	/* See gsw_pce_table_write for the rationale for this re-seed. */
-	gsw_r32_raw(cdev, PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_OFFSET, &ctrlval);
+	/* See gsw_pce_table_write for why the control word starts at zero. */
+	ctrlval = 0;
 
 	gsw_w32_raw(cdev, PCE_TBL_ADDR_ADDR_OFFSET, ptdata->pcindex);
 	/*TABLE ADDRESS*/
@@ -326,12 +334,6 @@ int gsw_pce_table_key_read(void *cdev, pctbl_prog_t *ptdata)
 
 	CHECK_BUSY(PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_OFFSET, PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SHIFT,
 		   PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SIZE, RETURN_FROM_FUNCTION);
-	/* See gsw_pce_table_write for the rationale for this re-seed. */
-	gsw_r32_raw(cdev, PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_OFFSET, &ctrlval);
-
-	ctrlval = gsw_field_w32(ctrlval, PCE_TBL_CTRL_ADDR_SHIFT,
-				PCE_TBL_CTRL_ADDR_SIZE, ptdata->table);
-
 	/*KEY REG*/
 	j = gswdev->pce_tbl_info[ptdata->table].num_key;
 
@@ -339,6 +341,11 @@ int gsw_pce_table_key_read(void *cdev, pctbl_prog_t *ptdata)
 		gsw_w32_raw(cdev, gswdev->pce_tbl_reg.key[i], ptdata->key[i]);
 	}
 
+	/* See gsw_pce_table_write for why the control word starts at zero. */
+	ctrlval = 0;
+
+	ctrlval = gsw_field_w32(ctrlval, PCE_TBL_CTRL_ADDR_SHIFT,
+				PCE_TBL_CTRL_ADDR_SIZE, ptdata->table);
 	ctrlval = gsw_field_w32(ctrlval, PCE_TBL_CTRL_OPMOD_SHIFT,
 				PCE_TBL_CTRL_OPMOD_SIZE, PCE_OP_MODE_KSRD);
 	ctrlval = gsw_field_w32(ctrlval, PCE_TBL_CTRL_KEYFORM_SHIFT,
@@ -404,8 +411,8 @@ int gsw_pce_table_key_write(void *cdev, pctbl_prog_t *ptdata)
 
 	CHECK_BUSY(PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_OFFSET, PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SHIFT,
 		   PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SIZE, RETURN_FROM_FUNCTION);
-	/* See gsw_pce_table_write for the rationale for this re-seed. */
-	gsw_r32_raw(cdev, PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_OFFSET, &ctrlval);
+	/* See gsw_pce_table_write for why the control word starts at zero. */
+	ctrlval = 0;
 
 	/*TABLE ADDRESS*/
 	ctrlval = gsw_field_w32(ctrlval, PCE_TBL_CTRL_ADDR_SHIFT,
